"== Sketch II #01 =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A crow lands on a branch, drawn from six quick overlapping gesture positions with only the final head and beak resolved in dark confident ink. Quick, alive, raw. In the right panel it is on a man, outer forearm. Create exceptional contemporary Sketch tattoo artwork where the visible drawing process is the defining visual language. THE DRAWING PROCESS IS THE STYLE. THE DRAWING LOOKS UNFINISHED. THE ARTWORK IS COMPLETELY INTENTIONAL. Do not create a polished tattoo and surround it with random scratches. Do not create ordinary Fine Line artwork with meaningless loose marks. Every unfinished mark must have a visual reason to exist. Use searching contours, repeated attempts, gesture lines, anatomical construction, centerlines, perspective guides, circles, ellipses, proportion marks, abandoned positions, overshooting strokes, hatching, cross-hatching, scribble shading, charcoal-like darkness, broken contours and selective areas of confident finished drawing. SOME LINES SEARCH. SOME LINES MEASURE. SOME LINES MOVE. SOME LINES COMMIT. Allow the viewer to understand how the artist discovered the final image. Use hierarchy. Not every mark should have equal strength. Preliminary construction can be lighter. Corrections can become more assertive. Final committed areas can use darker confident black. Allow some areas to remain almost unresolved while focal areas become sharply understandable. Movement is a major opportunity. Use repeated positions, ghosted limbs, abandoned wing locations, gestural arcs and overlapping anatomy to show where a body, animal or object has just been and where it is moving. Do not use generic cartoon speed lines. Sketch is a visual language, not a subject category. The subject can be anything. Portraits. Human figures. Hands. Animals. Birds. Horses. Dragons. Skulls. Skeletons. Demons. Angels. Dark fantasy. Gothic architecture. Mythological creatures. Horror. Botanicals. Machines. Motorcycles. Cars. Fashion. Architecture. Anatomy. Surrealism. Impossible creatures. Abstract drawing. Do not keep the imagery polite. Some tattoos should be beautiful. Some should be intimate. Some should be elegant. Some should be dark. Some should be aggressive. Some should be disturbing. Some should feel enormous and mythological. Some should make the viewer wonder what the hell they are looking at before the subject suddenly resolves. Push artistic ambition rather than novelty. Do not turn dark artwork into cheap gore or juvenile shock imagery. Darkness should come through composition, anatomy, mythology, mortality, psychological tension, scale, atmosphere and drawing energy. Black and natural skin should dominate much of the collection. Color may be used as part of the drawing process. Pale blue can represent preliminary construction. Red or burgundy can represent correction, alternative positioning or an additional drawing layer. Black can establish the final committed drawing. These relationships are possibilities rather than mandatory rules. Color must not become random decoration. Do not turn Sketch into Watercolour. Do not use full realistic color unless the concept specifically requires a hybrid. Hatching and cross-hatching should describe form and directional shadow. Scribble and charcoal-like marks should carry weight, movement or atmosphere rather than merely making the tattoo look dirty. Construction lines should relate logically to the subject. Human heads may use cranial masses, centerlines and eye axes. Hands may use joint circles and finger direction. Animals may expose skeletal or muscular construction. Machines and architecture may retain perspective axes and ellipses. Dragons and invented creatures should be constructed convincingly enough that their imaginary anatomy feels studied. Design every composition specifically for the stated body placement. THE BODY IS THE PAGE, BUT THE PAGE IS NOT FLAT. Use forearms for elongated drawings, perspective, smaller portraits and technical studies. Use upper arms and shoulders for portraits, creatures and compositions that benefit from wrapping construction. Use thighs for large figures, dark fantasy, horses, dragons and complicated drawing studies. Use calves for strong vertical compositions. Use ribs for gesture and elongated human figures. Use backs for rare major pieces where wings, anatomy, architecture or large drawing systems need space. Let construction and movement respond to body curvature rather than appearing printed onto the skin. Do not make every design huge. Most should remain wearable small and medium pieces. Use large scale where the artistic idea genuinely benefits from it. Reserve full backs, chest compositions and major limb projects for exceptional concepts. Avoid repetitive wolves, lions, roses and womens faces unless the treatment is genuinely exceptional. Avoid random sketch marks. Avoid fake handwritten notes. Avoid meaningless arrows. Avoid decorative geometry unrelated to construction. Avoid generic splatter. Avoid clean finished tattoos with superficial scribbles around them. Avoid making every drawing equally chaotic. Contrast beautifully resolved areas against unresolved areas. THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A BAD SKETCH AND A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO IS INTENTION. A BAD SKETCH LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE IT WAS NOT FINISHED. A GREAT SKETCH TATTOO LOOKS UNFINISHED BECAUSE THAT IS THE FINISHED ART. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
